#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""inbox-delivery — the in-sandbox delivery daemon.

Watches two inbound trees the runtime writes and performs the LAST HOP into a
live Claude Code session over its Unix domain socket. No router speaks UDS —
a socket is reachable only from inside the target's namespace — so this
process lives in the container and does what no router can.

Two lanes, different treatment, and the difference is the whole design:

  MAIL  (~/.handoff/inbox/)   a notice produces a CONTENT-FREE doorbell —
                              "you have mail; use the inbox read tool".
                              Bodies never cross this lane. No outcome file.
  PEER  (~/.handoff/peer/)    notice + body are injected as a teammate's
                              request — authorised by the router, evidenced
                              by the read-only mount (ruling 16) — with an
                              outcome written back for the router.

Both trees are READ-ONLY mounts. This daemon never creates, moves, renames or
deletes anything under them; there is no processed/ anywhere. At-most-once
rests on ONE record — the private delivered ledger under
AMAP_DELIVERY_STATE_DIR, keyed (tree, notice_id), because notice ids are only
unique per tree. It is written BEFORE the delivered outcome, survives
restarts, and is never derived from outcome files.

Contract — eight REQUIRED, no defaults; a missing or unreadable one is a clean
nonzero exit at start, never a guessed path used silently:

  AMAP_DELIVERY_MAIL_NOTICE_DIR   AMAP_DELIVERY_MAIL_CLAIM
  AMAP_DELIVERY_PEER_NOTICE_DIR   AMAP_DELIVERY_PEER_MESSAGE_DIR
  AMAP_DELIVERY_PEER_CLAIM        AMAP_DELIVERY_STATE_DIR
  AMAP_DELIVERY_OUTCOME_DIR       AMAP_DELIVERY_SESSION_SOURCE

AMAP_DELIVERY_SELF — this agent's own address, optional; see SELF_ENV. Used
for the doorbell's from-name and the peer lane's to==self integrity check.
Absent, the doorbell uses a constant and the peer lane refuses, saying why.

THIS DAEMON READS NO POLICY FILE. It once read `self` out of the adapter's
peers.json, and AMAP_DELIVERY_PEERS was required to name it; both are gone.
Who may task this agent is the router's decision, and a notice's presence in
the read-only peer tree is the router's signature on it (ruling 16). Nothing
here is an allowlist, and there is no longer a file that could be mistaken
for one.

This daemon knows no host variable and derives nothing from one. The host's
wrapper does that translation (ruling 15); a former cross-check against the
host's own variables compared values against their own source.

Claims: both are taken first, with the primitive in bin/_inboxlib.py, and
EITHER being held is a nonzero exit naming the holder. For a daemon there is
no "continue without the interlock".

Targeting — refuse, don't guess: run AMAP_DELIVERY_SESSION_SOURCE and count the
rows whose agent is "claude". Exactly one -> inject via its socket and key
file. Several -> outcome "ambiguous_target", the notice stays in the spool.
None -> wait and re-poll. "No panes ever" is not special-cased: not running
this daemon where no session can exist is the host's job.

The envelope — this daemon OWNS it. Claude Code parses the sender's identity
and class claim out of the CONTENT STRING with an anchored regex, measured
against a live session. So a body is never sent as the whole content: every
injected string is

    <cross-session-message from-name="<addr-spec>">\\n<body>\\n</cross-session-message>

which makes an envelope-shaped body just text, since the regex admits one
envelope. from-name is the runtime-asserted notice.from on the peer lane.
from-mode is NEVER set: claiming "bypass" would be asserting a class this
process does not have, and would turn a missing receiver setting into a
silent success instead of the `held` outcome that says the sandbox is
misconfigured.

Wire: NDJSON — an auth frame carrying the peerToken
from the key file, then ONE user frame with a msg_id and a `from`. RECEIPTS
ARE OUT OF BAND (measured again on 2.1.263, 2026-09-07):
the receiver CONNECTS OUTWARD to the socket named in `from` and writes
peer_message_status frames there. Nothing ever comes back on the inbound
connection — under `refuse` it simply stalls open. So this daemon binds a
listening reply socket BEFORE it sends, one per in-flight message, in the
receiver's own socket directory with a basename the receiver accepts
(REPLY_SOCK_RE), advertises it as `from: "uds:<path>"`, and reads receipts
there. The per-message socket is the correlation: receipts carry no
orig_msg_id.

Under `accept` the receiver emits NO receipt on either channel; a successful
delivery is visible only in the receiver's own log. Therefore SILENCE within
the receipt window is `delivered` — the frame was accepted and no negative
receipt arrived — recorded with a `detail` that says exactly that, and it is
NEVER retried (a retry under `accept` would deliver the task again; that was
the bug ruling 14 fixed). Negative receipts are the observable ones:
`expired`+`status_detail: refused` (or `refused`) means the receiving sandbox
is not accepting cross-session messages — a configuration hold, reported as
outcome `held` with an operator-facing detail and re-offered on the next
daemon life; `held` is a human approval pending; `denied` a human said no;
`dropped` the receiver dropped the frame, retried with backoff.

AMAP_DELIVERY_RECEIPT_WINDOW_SECONDS is the ONE optional variable: how long
silence must last before it counts as delivered (default 30; refusals arrive
in about a millisecond and hold receipts within seconds). Tests set it low.

Outcomes: one file per outcome under AMAP_DELIVERY_OUTCOME_DIR named
<tree>-<notice_id>.json — today always peer-<id>.json, since the mail lane
writes none. EXACTLY these keys and no others:

    {"outcome": "delivered|held|denied|refused|ambiguous_target|inject_failed",
     "ts": "<ISO 8601 UTC, seconds, Z>", "tree": "peer",
     "notice_id": "<32 hex, = the filename's>", "detail": "<optional>"}

Written as .peer-<id>.json.tmp then os.rename — the router reads only names
matching ^peer-[a-f0-9]{32}\\.json$, so the tmp name MUST keep its leading dot
and .tmp suffix; never os.link (the router requires nlink == 1); mode 0644.
THE ROUTER UNLINKS EACH FILE AFTER READING IT: never assume a previous outcome
is still there, never read outcomes back, and on a later transition for the
same notice write the same name again. The outcomes directory is created on
first write — the outbox is rw and the router does not pre-create ext/.

Stdlib only. Everything this process says goes to stderr; stdout is unused.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import importlib.util
import json
import os
import re
import signal
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import threading
import time
import uuid
from typing import Dict, List, Optional, Tuple

NAME = "inbox-delivery"
CONSUMER_NAME = "inbox-delivery"

ENV_KEYS = (
    "AMAP_DELIVERY_MAIL_NOTICE_DIR", "AMAP_DELIVERY_MAIL_CLAIM",
    "AMAP_DELIVERY_PEER_NOTICE_DIR", "AMAP_DELIVERY_PEER_MESSAGE_DIR",
    "AMAP_DELIVERY_PEER_CLAIM", "AMAP_DELIVERY_STATE_DIR",
    "AMAP_DELIVERY_OUTCOME_DIR", "AMAP_DELIVERY_SESSION_SOURCE",
)

TREE_MAIL = "mail"
TREE_PEER = "peer"

OUTCOMES = ("delivered", "held", "denied", "refused", "ambiguous_target", "inject_failed")
TERMINAL_RECEIPTS = {"delivered", "denied"}

POLL_SECONDS = 0.5
HEARTBEAT_SECONDS = 15.0
RECEIPT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 30.0      # default receipt window; see RECEIPT_WINDOW_ENV
RECEIPT_WINDOW_ENV = "AMAP_DELIVERY_RECEIPT_WINDOW_SECONDS"   # OPTIONAL tuning knob

# This agent's own address, and the ONE variable that is optional without
# being a tuning knob. It replaced `self` in peers.json, which was the last
# field this daemon read from that file; that reader is gone and
# AMAP_DELIVERY_PEERS left the required set with it.
#
# ABSENT IS A SUPPORTED DEPLOYMENT, not a misconfiguration to be crashed on.
# A fleet that declares no fleet domain has no peer lane and no address to
# name, and the mail lane does not need one — the doorbell falls back to a
# constant. Requiring this would take the daemon away from every mail-only
# fleet to protect a lane they do not run. The daemon cannot tell that case
# apart from a peer fleet that forgot to export it (Config requires the peer
# directories either way), so it declines to guess and stays useful to both.
#
# What makes that safe is that the peer lane's failure WITHOUT this is loud in
# three places, not silent: a line at startup, an outcome file per refused
# notice, and the DSN the router raises from it. Fail-closed AND legible is
# the bar; fail-closed and quiet is the `_admit` defect this file's history
# is built around.
#
# PRESENT-BUT-MALFORMED STILL DIES. A misspelling is not an omission: acting
# on it would deliver under an address nobody named.
SELF_ENV = "AMAP_DELIVERY_SELF"
# The receiver honours a reply address only when its basename has this shape
# and it sits inside a socket directory the receiver recognises (CROSS_SESSION_INBOUND §6a).
REPLY_SOCK_RE = re.compile(r"^(\d+(-[0-9a-f]{8})?|[0-9a-f]{1,16})\.sock$")
HELD_WAIT_SECONDS = 3600.0          # how long a worker waits on a held receipt
BACKOFF_FIRST, BACKOFF_MAX = 2.0, 300.0
MAX_BODY_BYTES = 256 * 1024         # a body larger than this is refused, not truncated

NOTICE_ID_RE = re.compile(r"^[a-f0-9]{32}$")
NOTICE_FILE_RE = re.compile(r"^notice-([a-f0-9]{32})\.json$")
# A bare addr-spec, the shape peers.json's `self` and notice.from carry.
# Deliberately strict: it is compared for equality, never parsed further.
ADDR_SPEC_RE = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+$")

DOORBELL = "you have mail; use the inbox read tool"


# --- logging -----------------------------------------------------------------

def log(msg: str) -> None:
    print(f"{NAME}: {msg}", file=sys.stderr, flush=True)


def die(msg: str, code: int = 2) -> "NoReturn":
    log(f"fatal: {msg}")
    sys.exit(code)


def utc_now() -> str:
    return time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())


# --- the shared module, loaded by path ---------------------------------------

def load_inboxlib():
    """bin/_inboxlib.py from beside THIS file — no package, no install step,
    no sys.path from the environment."""
    here = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
    path = os.path.join(here, "_inboxlib.py")
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("_inboxlib", path)
    if spec is None or spec.loader is None:
        die(f"cannot load the shared module at {path}")
    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    spec.loader.exec_module(mod)
    return mod


# --- configuration -----------------------------------------------------------

class Config:
    """The eight required variables, validated once at start. Attribute names are the
    variable names minus the prefix, lower-cased."""

    def __init__(self, env: Dict[str, str]):
        missing = [k for k in ENV_KEYS if not (env.get(k) or "").strip()]
        if missing:
            die("missing required environment: " + ", ".join(missing)
                + " — every AMAP_DELIVERY_* variable is required; there are no "
                  "defaults, because a guessed path is a wrong path used silently")
        for k in ENV_KEYS:
            setattr(self, k[len("AMAP_DELIVERY_"):].lower(), env[k].strip())
        raw = (env.get(RECEIPT_WINDOW_ENV) or "").strip()
        self.receipt_window = RECEIPT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS
        if raw:
            try:
                v = float(raw)
            except ValueError:
                v = -1.0
            if not v > 0:
                die(f"{RECEIPT_WINDOW_ENV} must be a positive number of seconds, got {raw!r}")
            self.receipt_window = v
        # Validated HERE, at startup, with the rest of the config — not at
        # first use. A malformed address would otherwise surface as a peer
        # lane that refuses everything, which reads as a router fault.
        raw_self = (env.get(SELF_ENV) or "").strip()
        self.self_address = raw_self or None
        if raw_self and not ADDR_SPEC_RE.match(raw_self):
            die(f"{SELF_ENV}={raw_self!r} is not a bare addr-spec "
                f"(local@domain). Refusing to start: a misspelling is not an "
                f"omission, and continuing would run this agent under an "
                f"address nobody named")

    def check_readable(self) -> None:
        """Die at start if anything that must already exist does not."""
        for label, path in (("mail notice dir", self.mail_notice_dir),
                            ("peer notice dir", self.peer_notice_dir),
                            ("peer message dir", self.peer_message_dir)):
            if not os.path.isdir(path):
                die(f"{label} {path} is not a directory")
            if not os.access(path, os.R_OK | os.X_OK):
                die(f"{label} {path} is not readable")
        if not (os.path.isfile(self.session_source)
                and os.access(self.session_source, os.X_OK)):
            die(f"session source {self.session_source} is not an executable file")
        for label, path in (("state dir", self.state_dir),
                            ("mail claim", os.path.dirname(self.mail_claim)),
                            ("peer claim", os.path.dirname(self.peer_claim))):
            try:
                os.makedirs(path, exist_ok=True)
            except OSError as e:
                die(f"{label} {path} cannot be created: {e}")


# --- atomic files ------------------------------------------------------------

def atomic_write_json(path: str, doc: dict, tmp_name: str, mode: int = 0o644) -> None:
    """Write `doc` to `tmp_name` (a sibling of `path`), fsync, then rename into
    place. Never os.link — the reader requires nlink == 1."""
    d = os.path.dirname(path)
    tmp = os.path.join(d, tmp_name)
    fd = os.open(tmp, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_TRUNC, mode)
    try:
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write(json.dumps(doc, sort_keys=True))
            f.flush()
            os.fsync(f.fileno())
        os.chmod(tmp, mode)
        os.rename(tmp, path)
    except BaseException:
        try:
            os.unlink(tmp)
        except OSError:
            pass
        raise


# --- the ledger: the ONLY at-most-once record --------------------------------

class Ledger:
    """One empty marker per (tree, notice_id) under <state>/ledger/. Existence is
    the record; the file is created O_EXCL so a crash mid-write cannot leave a
    half-record that reads as 'delivered'."""

    def __init__(self, state_dir: str):
        self.dir = os.path.join(state_dir, "ledger")
        os.makedirs(self.dir, exist_ok=True)

    def _path(self, tree: str, notice_id: str) -> str:
        return os.path.join(self.dir, f"{tree}-{notice_id}")

    def has(self, tree: str, notice_id: str) -> bool:
        return os.path.exists(self._path(tree, notice_id))

    def record(self, tree: str, notice_id: str) -> None:
        try:
            fd = os.open(self._path(tree, notice_id), os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL, 0o644)
            os.close(fd)
        except FileExistsError:
            pass


# --- outcomes ------------------------------------------------------------------

class Outcomes:
    def __init__(self, outcome_dir: str):
        self.dir = outcome_dir

    def write(self, tree: str, notice_id: str, outcome: str, detail: Optional[str] = None) -> None:
        assert outcome in OUTCOMES, outcome
        try:
            os.makedirs(self.dir, exist_ok=True)
        except OSError as e:
            log(f"cannot create outcome dir {self.dir}: {e}")
            return
        doc = {"outcome": outcome, "ts": utc_now(), "tree": tree, "notice_id": notice_id}
        if detail:
            doc["detail"] = str(detail)[:500]
        final = os.path.join(self.dir, f"{tree}-{notice_id}.json")
        # Leading dot + .tmp: a name the router's ^peer-[a-f0-9]{32}\.json$
        # hygiene excludes, so it never reads a partial file.
        tmp_name = f".{tree}-{notice_id}.json.tmp"
        try:
            atomic_write_json(final, doc, tmp_name)
        except OSError as e:
            log(f"cannot write outcome {outcome} for {tree}/{notice_id}: {e}")


# --- allowed senders -----------------------------------------------------------

# Must equal the router's ROUTER_LOCAL_PART (`router/config.py` in the local router). Not imported: the daemon
# has no dependency on the router's code, and the value is a protocol
# constant, not a configuration.
ROUTER_LOCAL_PART = "amap.router"


# --- notices ---------------------------------------------------------------------

def list_notices(notice_dir: str) -> List[Tuple[str, str]]:
    """[(notice_id, path)] for every well-named notice file. Never writes."""
    out = []
    try:
        names = os.listdir(notice_dir)
    except OSError as e:
        log(f"cannot list {notice_dir}: {e}")
        return out
    for name in sorted(names):
        m = NOTICE_FILE_RE.match(name)
        if m:
            out.append((m.group(1), os.path.join(notice_dir, name)))
    return out


def read_json(path: str, limit: int = MAX_BODY_BYTES) -> Optional[dict]:
    try:
        if os.path.getsize(path) > limit:
            return None
        with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            doc = json.load(f)
    except (OSError, ValueError, UnicodeDecodeError):
        return None
    return doc if isinstance(doc, dict) else None


def sanitize_attr(value: str, limit: int = 200) -> str:
    """An attribute value inside the envelope tag: printable, no quotes, no
    newlines, bounded. addr-specs never contain any of those; this is the
    belt behind that brace."""
    s = "".join(c for c in str(value) if c.isprintable() and c not in '"\\\r\n')
    return s[:limit]


def envelope(from_name: str, body: str) -> str:
    """The daemon's OWN envelope around every injected body — see the module
    docstring. Never from-mode."""
    return f'<cross-session-message from-name="{sanitize_attr(from_name)}">\n{body}\n</cross-session-message>'


# --- targeting -------------------------------------------------------------------

class Target:
    __slots__ = ("socket_path", "key_file")

    def __init__(self, socket_path: str, key_file: str):
        self.socket_path = socket_path
        self.key_file = key_file


def find_claude_targets(session_source: str) -> Optional[List[Target]]:
    """Run the helper; one row per live pane:
    `agent pane_index pane_pid agent_pid socket keyfile`. Returns the claude
    rows, or None if the helper itself failed (which is not 'zero panes')."""
    try:
        p = subprocess.run([session_source], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=10)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as e:
        log(f"session source failed: {e}")
        return None
    if p.returncode != 0:
        log(f"session source exited {p.returncode}: {p.stderr.strip()[:200]}")
        return None
    targets = []
    for line in p.stdout.splitlines():
        parts = line.split()
        if len(parts) >= 6 and parts[0] == "claude":
            targets.append(Target(parts[4], parts[5]))
    return targets


# --- the wire ----------------------------------------------------------------------

class InjectResult:
    __slots__ = ("status", "detail")

    def __init__(self, status: str, detail: str = ""):
        self.status = status      # a receipt status, or "no_receipt", or "error"
        self.detail = detail


def _read_token(key_file: str) -> Optional[str]:
    doc = read_json(key_file, limit=64 * 1024)
    tok = doc.get("peerToken") if doc else None
    return tok if isinstance(tok, str) and tok else None


class ReplySocket:
    """The daemon's OWN listening socket for receipts — bound BEFORE the frame
    is sent, advertised in the frame's `from`, one per in-flight message (the
    socket is the correlation; receipts carry no orig_msg_id). It lives in the
    receiver's socket directory because the receiver only honours a reply
    address inside a directory it recognises, with a basename matching
    REPLY_SOCK_RE — otherwise it logs "reply address unshaped" and sends no
    receipt at all."""

    def __init__(self, socket_dir: str):
        self.path = os.path.join(socket_dir, uuid.uuid4().hex[:16] + ".sock")
        assert REPLY_SOCK_RE.match(os.path.basename(self.path))
        try:
            os.unlink(self.path)
        except FileNotFoundError:
            pass
        self.srv = socket.socket(socket.AF_UNIX, socket.SOCK_STREAM)
        self.srv.bind(self.path)
        os.chmod(self.path, 0o600)
        self.srv.listen(8)
        self.srv.setblocking(False)
        self.conns: List[socket.socket] = []
        self.bufs: Dict[int, bytes] = {}

    def receipts(self, deadline: List[float], stop: threading.Event):
        """Yield every parsed frame that arrives before deadline[0] (a list so
        the consumer can extend it on `held`) or until `stop`."""
        import select
        while not stop.is_set() and time.monotonic() < deadline[0]:
            wait = min(0.25, max(0.0, deadline[0] - time.monotonic()))
            try:
                ready, _, _ = select.select([self.srv] + self.conns, [], [], wait)
            except (OSError, ValueError):
                return
            for r in ready:
                if r is self.srv:
                    try:
                        c, _ = self.srv.accept()
                    except OSError:
                        continue
                    c.setblocking(False)
                    self.conns.append(c)
                    self.bufs[c.fileno()] = b""
                    continue
                try:
                    chunk = r.recv(65536)
                except OSError:
                    chunk = b""
                if not chunk:
                    self.conns.remove(r)
                    self.bufs.pop(r.fileno(), None)
                    try:
                        r.close()
                    except OSError:
                        pass
                    continue
                buf = self.bufs.get(r.fileno(), b"") + chunk
                while b"\n" in buf:
                    line, buf = buf.split(b"\n", 1)
                    try:
                        frame = json.loads(line.decode("utf-8"))
                    except ValueError:
                        continue
                    if isinstance(frame, dict):
                        yield frame
                self.bufs[r.fileno()] = buf

    def close(self) -> None:
        for c in self.conns:
            try:
                c.close()
            except OSError:
                pass
        try:
            self.srv.close()
        except OSError:
            pass
        try:
            os.unlink(self.path)
        except OSError:
            pass


def _held_detail(reason: str) -> str:
    """What a `held` outcome tells the operator. NOT "a human will approve":
    measured 2026-09-10, a hold caused by a crossSessionInbound SETTING returns
    the same generic reason as a permission-mode hold ("held for the recipient
    user's approval"), and the wire carries no cause. Only two of the receiver's
    eight causes (mode-mismatch, no-mode-asserted) present an approval dialog;
    the six setting/policy causes park the message until a setting changes and
    no approval can release them. So quote the receiver and say plainly that the
    cause is not knowable from here."""
    said = f' It says: "{reason[:160]}".' if reason else ""
    return ("held by the receiving sandbox." + said +
            " The wire carries no hold cause: a permission-mode hold is"
            " human-approvable, a crossSessionInbound setting hold is not."
            " Check that sandbox.")


def inject(target: Target, content: str, msg_id: str,
           on_status, stop: threading.Event,
           first_timeout: float = RECEIPT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS,
           held_wait: float = HELD_WAIT_SECONDS) -> InjectResult:
    """Bind the reply socket, send auth + one user frame naming it in `from`,
    then read receipts FROM THE REPLY SOCKET (never from the inbound
    connection, which stays silent — module docstring).

    Returns one of: "silent" (no receipt within first_timeout — under `accept`
    this IS delivery), "delivered", "denied", "held" (waited out),
    "receiver_refused" (the receiving sandbox does not accept cross-session
    messages), "dropped", "expired", or "error" (could not bind, connect or
    send). `on_status(status, reason)` fires for every receipt so a `held` can be
    written as an outcome while this call keeps waiting for the transition —
    a receipt listener has to be long-lived (uds_findings §8)."""
    token = _read_token(target.key_file)
    if token is None:
        return InjectResult("error", f"key file {target.key_file} unreadable or has no peerToken")
    reply = None
    inbound = None
    try:
        try:
            reply = ReplySocket(os.path.dirname(target.socket_path))
        except OSError as e:
            return InjectResult("error", f"cannot bind reply socket: {e}")
        try:
            inbound = socket.socket(socket.AF_UNIX, socket.SOCK_STREAM)
            inbound.settimeout(min(first_timeout, 10.0))
            inbound.connect(target.socket_path)
            inbound.sendall((json.dumps({"type": "auth", "token": token}) + "\n").encode("utf-8"))
            inbound.sendall((json.dumps({
                "type": "user", "from": f"uds:{reply.path}", "msg_id": msg_id,
                "message": {"role": "user", "content": content},
            }) + "\n").encode("utf-8"))
        except OSError as e:
            return InjectResult("error", f"connect/send failed: {e}")

        deadline = [time.monotonic() + first_timeout]
        last = "silent"
        for frame in reply.receipts(deadline, stop):
            if frame.get("type") != "control" or frame.get("action") != "peer_message_status":
                continue
            status = str(frame.get("status") or "")
            detail = str(frame.get("status_detail") or "")
            reason = str(frame.get("reason") or frame.get("drop_reason") or "")
            if status == "refused" or (status == "expired" and detail == "refused"):
                on_status("receiver_refused", reason)
                return InjectResult("receiver_refused", reason)
            last = status or last
            on_status(status, reason)
            if status in TERMINAL_RECEIPTS:
                return InjectResult(status, reason)
            if status == "held":
                deadline[0] = time.monotonic() + held_wait     # not terminal: keep listening
            elif status in ("dropped", "expired"):
                return InjectResult(status, reason)
        return InjectResult(last)
    finally:
        if reply is not None:
            reply.close()
        if inbound is not None:
            try:
                inbound.close()
            except OSError:
                pass


# --- the daemon ------------------------------------------------------------------------

class Daemon:
    def __init__(self, cfg: Config, lib):
        self.cfg = cfg
        self.lib = lib
        self.stop = threading.Event()
        self.ledger = Ledger(cfg.state_dir)
        self.outcomes = Outcomes(cfg.outcome_dir)
        self.window = cfg.receipt_window
        self.claims: Dict[str, object] = {}
        self.started_at = utc_now()
        # per (tree, id): in-flight worker, last outcome written, next retry time
        self.in_flight: set = set()
        self.last_outcome: Dict[Tuple[str, str], str] = {}
        self.retry_at: Dict[Tuple[str, str], float] = {}
        self.backoff: Dict[Tuple[str, str], float] = {}
        self.lock = threading.Lock()

    # -- lifecycle ------------------------------------------------------------

    def take_claims(self) -> None:
        for tree, claim_path, notice_dir in (
                (TREE_MAIL, self.cfg.mail_claim, self.cfg.mail_notice_dir),
                (TREE_PEER, self.cfg.peer_claim, self.cfg.peer_notice_dir)):
            try:
                self.claims[tree] = self.lib.acquire_consumer_claim(
                    claim_path, notice_dir, CONSUMER_NAME, log=log)
            except self.lib.ClaimError as e:
                # Release whatever we already hold, then refuse. For a daemon
                # there is no continuing without the interlock.
                for h in self.claims.values():
                    h.release()
                die(f"{tree} claim: {e}")

    def release_claims(self) -> None:
        for h in self.claims.values():
            h.release()
        self.claims.clear()

    def heartbeat(self) -> None:
        doc = {
            "pid": os.getpid(),
            "started_at": self.started_at,
            "claims": {TREE_MAIL: self.cfg.mail_claim, TREE_PEER: self.cfg.peer_claim},
            "heartbeat_at": utc_now(),
        }
        try:
            atomic_write_json(os.path.join(self.cfg.state_dir, "daemon.json"), doc, ".daemon.json.tmp")
        except OSError as e:
            log(f"heartbeat write failed: {e}")

    def heartbeat_loop(self) -> None:
        while not self.stop.is_set():
            self.heartbeat()
            self.stop.wait(HEARTBEAT_SECONDS)

    # -- targeting ------------------------------------------------------------

    def one_target(self, tree: str, notice_id: str) -> Optional[Target]:
        """Exactly one claude row, or None (with the reason handled here)."""
        targets = find_claude_targets(self.cfg.session_source)
        if targets is None:
            return None                       # helper failed: try again later
        if len(targets) == 0:
            return None                       # no session yet: wait
        if len(targets) > 1:
            if tree == TREE_PEER:
                self._outcome_once(tree, notice_id, "ambiguous_target",
                                   f"{len(targets)} live claude sessions; refusing to guess")
            return None
        return targets[0]

    def _outcome_once(self, tree: str, notice_id: str, outcome: str, detail: str = "") -> None:
        """Write an outcome when it is a TRANSITION for this notice. The router
        dedups per (tree, id, outcome) and unlinks, so rewriting the same state
        every poll would only churn; a later different state is always written."""
        key = (tree, notice_id)
        with self.lock:
            if self.last_outcome.get(key) == outcome:
                return
            self.last_outcome[key] = outcome
        self.outcomes.write(tree, notice_id, outcome, detail or None)

    # -- mail lane ------------------------------------------------------------

    def mail_pass(self) -> None:
        for notice_id, _path in list_notices(self.cfg.mail_notice_dir):
            key = (TREE_MAIL, notice_id)
            if self.ledger.has(TREE_MAIL, notice_id) or key in self.in_flight:
                continue
            if not self._retry_due(key):
                continue
            target = self.one_target(TREE_MAIL, notice_id)
            if target is None:
                continue
            me = self.cfg.self_address or "inbox"
            # Content-free by design: no field of the notice enters the string.
            content = envelope(me, DOORBELL)
            self._spawn(key, target, content, is_mail=True)

    # -- peer lane ------------------------------------------------------------

    def peer_pass(self) -> None:
        for notice_id, path in list_notices(self.cfg.peer_notice_dir):
            key = (TREE_PEER, notice_id)
            if self.ledger.has(TREE_PEER, notice_id) or key in self.in_flight:
                continue
            if not self._retry_due(key):
                continue
            verdict = self._admit(notice_id, path)
            if verdict is None:
                continue
            sender, body = verdict
            target = self.one_target(TREE_PEER, notice_id)
            if target is None:
                continue
            self._spawn(key, target, envelope(sender, body), is_mail=False)

    def _admit(self, notice_id: str, path: str) -> Optional[Tuple[str, str]]:
        """The admission gate. Returns (from, body) to inject, or None
        after writing `refused`. Every failure is fail-closed."""
        notice = read_json(path)
        if notice is None:
            return self._refuse(notice_id, "notice unreadable or not an object")
        if notice.get("notice_id") != notice_id:
            return self._refuse(notice_id, "notice_id does not match the filename")
        if notice.get("kind") != TREE_PEER:
            return self._refuse(notice_id, f"kind is {notice.get('kind')!r}, not 'peer'")
        msg = notice.get("message")
        if not isinstance(msg, dict):
            return self._refuse(notice_id, "message is not an object")
        frm = msg.get("from")
        if not (isinstance(frm, str) and ADDR_SPEC_RE.match(frm)):
            return self._refuse(notice_id, "message.from is not an addr-spec")
        # No allowlist (ruling 16). This notice is in a tree only the host can
        # write, so the router put it here, and the router is the authority on
        # who may task whom. Two INTEGRITY checks remain, neither of which is
        # a second opinion on that policy:
        me = self.cfg.self_address
        if me is None:
            # Names the variable, because this detail is the whole diagnostic:
            # the router forwards no detail into its DSN, so the sender learns
            # only that it was refused. An operator reading the outcome file
            # should not have to guess which of the two lanes is unconfigured.
            return self._refuse(
                notice_id,
                f"${SELF_ENV} is not set, so this agent does not know its own "
                f"address and cannot check that the notice is addressed to it")
        if frm.split("@", 1)[0] == ROUTER_LOCAL_PART:
            # The router does not task agents; its own notices arrive on the
            # MAIL lane as delivery status. A peer notice claiming to be from
            # it is a fault, and treating it as a task would inject the
            # router's status text as a teammate's request.
            return self._refuse(notice_id, f"sender {frm} is the router, which never tasks")
        body_doc = read_json(os.path.join(self.cfg.peer_message_dir, f"notice-{notice_id}.json"))
        if body_doc is None:
            return self._refuse(notice_id, "body spool missing, unreadable or oversized")
        # THE RECIPIENT CHECK READS THE BODY, NOT THE NOTICE.
        #
        # It used to read `message.to` off the notice, which the router never
        # writes: `deliver.py:_message_and_notice` builds that object with
        # id/from/subject/preview/mailbox and nothing else. `msg.get("to")` was
        # therefore always None, `None != me` always true, and EVERY peer
        # notice was refused — fail-closed, silently, for the whole life of the
        # lane. The sender got a DSN whose generic prose blamed an allowlist
        # that ruling 16 had already deleted, so the symptom pointed away from
        # the cause.
        #
        # Validation would not have caught it: since AMP 3.0.0 every
        # runtime-authored document is OPEN (§7 tolerance; `additionalProperties`
        # absent, `fixtures/valid/notice-unknown-member.json`), so a runtime
        # emitting `message.to` passes the gate clean and a reader of it sees
        # None against this one. Knowing WHICH DOCUMENT carries a field is not
        # something the schema will enforce for you.
        #
        # `to` lives on the message SPOOL doc, which is read just above. The
        # check itself is right and stays: a notice that landed in this tree
        # but is addressed elsewhere is a router fault, and injecting another
        # agent's task is wrong whoever authorised it.
        to = body_doc.get("to")
        if to != me:
            # NAMES THE DOCUMENT IT READ, and the value it found. The previous
            # wording — "message.to is not self (<me>)" — was identical to what
            # the BROKEN version emitted while reading the notice, so the one
            # artefact that survives a refusal could not distinguish "this
            # daemon is the old build" from "the body really is addressed
            # elsewhere". An operator holding that string learned nothing about
            # which, and the DSN forwards no detail at all, so this text is the
            # whole diagnostic.
            return self._refuse(
                notice_id,
                f"body spool `to` is {to!r}, not self ({me})")
        body = body_doc.get("body_text")
        if not isinstance(body, str):
            return self._refuse(notice_id, "body_text missing")
        atts = body_doc.get("attachments")
        if isinstance(atts, list) and atts:
            lines = []
            for a in atts[:50]:
                if isinstance(a, dict):
                    lines.append("- " + " ".join(
                        f"{k}={sanitize_attr(str(a.get(k, '')), 120)!r}"
                        for k in ("filename", "media_type", "size_bytes", "disposition")))
            body += "\n\nattachments (descriptors only; bytes via read_attachment):\n" + "\n".join(lines)
        return frm, body

    def _refuse(self, notice_id: str, why: str) -> None:
        log(f"peer notice {notice_id}: refused — {why}")
        # Refused is terminal for this notice: record it so it is not re-judged
        # every poll, then tell the sender through the outcome path.
        self.ledger.record(TREE_PEER, notice_id)
        self._outcome_once(TREE_PEER, notice_id, "refused", why)
        return None

    # -- injection workers ----------------------------------------------------

    def _retry_due(self, key) -> bool:
        return time.monotonic() >= self.retry_at.get(key, 0.0)

    def _schedule_retry(self, key) -> None:
        b = self.backoff.get(key, BACKOFF_FIRST)
        self.retry_at[key] = time.monotonic() + b
        self.backoff[key] = min(b * 2, BACKOFF_MAX)

    def _spawn(self, key, target: Target, content: str, *, is_mail: bool) -> None:
        self.in_flight.add(key)
        t = threading.Thread(target=self._deliver, args=(key, target, content, is_mail), daemon=True)
        t.start()

    def _deliver(self, key, target: Target, content: str, is_mail: bool) -> None:
        tree, notice_id = key
        msg_id = uuid.uuid4().hex
        try:
            if is_mail:
                # A doorbell is non-consuming and a duplicate is a non-event, so
                # a short receipt wait is enough; ledger on any accepted send.
                r = inject(target, content, msg_id, lambda s, r_: None, self.stop,
                           first_timeout=min(5.0, self.window), held_wait=min(5.0, self.window))
                if r.status == "error":
                    self._schedule_retry(key)
                    log(f"mail doorbell {notice_id}: inject failed ({r.detail}); will retry")
                    return
                if r.status == "receiver_refused":
                    # The sandbox refuses cross-session messages; ring again much later.
                    self.retry_at[key] = time.monotonic() + HELD_WAIT_SECONDS
                    log(f"mail doorbell {notice_id}: receiver refuses cross-session messages; "
                        f"will re-offer in {int(HELD_WAIT_SECONDS)}s")
                    return
                self.ledger.record(TREE_MAIL, notice_id)
                return

            def on_status(status: str, reason: str = "") -> None:
                if status == "held":
                    self._outcome_once(tree, notice_id, "held", _held_detail(reason))

            r = inject(target, content, msg_id, on_status, self.stop, first_timeout=self.window)
            if r.status == "silent":
                # accept emits no receipt on any channel (measured): the frame was
                # taken and nothing negative came back within the window. This IS
                # delivery, said honestly in the detail — and it is NEVER retried.
                self.ledger.record(tree, notice_id)          # BEFORE the outcome
                self._outcome_once(tree, notice_id, "delivered",
                                   f"inferred: receiver accepted the frame and issued no negative "
                                   f"receipt within {self.window:g}s (accept emits none)")
            elif r.status == "delivered":
                self.ledger.record(tree, notice_id)          # BEFORE the outcome
                self._outcome_once(tree, notice_id, "delivered", "receipt: delivered")
            elif r.status == "denied":
                self.ledger.record(tree, notice_id)          # a human said no; final
                self._outcome_once(tree, notice_id, "denied", r.detail)
            elif r.status == "receiver_refused":
                # A configuration hold, not a human decision and not this daemon's
                # gate: the receiving sandbox has crossSessionInbound off or set to
                # refuse. Alert the operator through `held`; re-offer much later.
                self._outcome_once(tree, notice_id, "held",
                                   "receiver refuses cross-session messages — crossSessionInbound is "
                                   "not `accept` in this sandbox; fix the setting and the daemon re-offers")
                self.retry_at[key] = time.monotonic() + HELD_WAIT_SECONDS
            elif r.status == "held":
                # Waited out; still held. Leave it: re-sending would park a
                # second copy. The next process life re-offers it.
                self.retry_at[key] = time.monotonic() + HELD_WAIT_SECONDS
            else:
                # error / dropped / expired: nothing was delivered; retry with backoff.
                self._outcome_once(tree, notice_id, "inject_failed", f"{r.status}: {r.detail}"[:200])
                self._schedule_retry(key)
        finally:
            self.in_flight.discard(key)

    # -- main -----------------------------------------------------------------

    def run(self) -> int:
        # HANDLERS FIRST, BEFORE ANY CLAIM IS TAKEN. Between taking the claims
        # and installing these, SIGTERM carries its default disposition: the
        # process dies without unwinding, the `finally` below never runs, and
        # both claim files are left behind holding an interlock nothing
        # released. The window is small enough that a fast machine never sees
        # it and a contended CI runner does.
        def on_term(signum, frame):
            self.stop.set()
        signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, on_term)
        signal.signal(signal.SIGINT, on_term)

        self.take_claims()
        self.heartbeat()
        hb = threading.Thread(target=self.heartbeat_loop, daemon=True)
        hb.start()

        log(f"started pid {os.getpid()}; mail={self.cfg.mail_notice_dir} peer={self.cfg.peer_notice_dir}")
        try:
            while not self.stop.is_set():
                try:
                    self.mail_pass()
                    self.peer_pass()
                except Exception as e:  # never let one bad poll kill the daemon
                    log(f"poll error: {e}")
                self.stop.wait(POLL_SECONDS)
        finally:
            self.release_claims()
            self.heartbeat()
            log("stopped")
        return 0


def main() -> int:
    env = dict(os.environ)
    cfg = Config(env)
    cfg.check_readable()
    lib = load_inboxlib()
    return Daemon(cfg, lib).run()


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
